WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:09.900
- Good evening everyone,
what an exciting day.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.570
It has been wonderful to
see NASA's rover, Perseverance

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.870
successfully landing on Mars.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.110
As we celebrate human, scientific

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:21.280
and technological
achievement, let us not forget

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.470
that we have the power to transform

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.770
not just the outer world
but our inner worlds too.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.500
And that brings me to
our distinguished speaker

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.220
and his talk today.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.530
Before introducing Marc Brackett,

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.170
our speaker for this evening,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.930
I first wanted to recognize the generosity

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.190
of Mr and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.640
whose support has enabled us to bring you

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.420
the (indistinct) and discussions.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.293
May I request everyone to mute please.

00:00:52.737 --> 00:00:57.140
I'm absolutely delighted to
see that they are here today

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.840
and I just wanted to formally remind you

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.770
that since we are recording this event,

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:07.190
I would like to request all
of you to mute your videos.

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:10.790
And we will have a brief
Q&amp;A following the talk

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:15.250
and I would like you to
kindly submit your questions

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:20.080
onto the chat and Dr. Ty Kamp
will pass on your questions

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.297
and manage the Q&amp;A.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.700
Now to our speaker this evening.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.300
It is my absolute pleasure to introduce

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.790
one of our own colleagues, Marc Brackett.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.000
Marc is the Founding Director of the Yale

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.070
Center for Emotional Intelligence

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.030
and a Professor in the Child Study Center

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.500
and at the Yale School of Medicine.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.710
His research has focused
on some of the very,

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.230
very big questions like
the role of emotions

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.300
and emotional intelligence
in learning, decision-making,

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.200
creativity, relationship
quality and mental health.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:59.270
And he's also thought a
lot about the measurement

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.420
of emotional intelligence
and the influences

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:06.860
that emotional intelligence
training has on the health

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:10.830
and performance particularly
workplace performance

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.440
and climate for adults
as well as the importance

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.570
of this training for young minds.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.730
He has published hundreds
of scholarly articles,

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:25.730
has been recipient of many, many awards,

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.550
including the Joseph Zins
Award for his research

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.750
on social and emotional learning.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.460
He also has an honorary doctorate

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.710
from Manhattanville College.

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.180
He's a distinguished scientist
on the National Commission

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.770
on Social, Emotional
and Academic Development

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:43.150
and on the board of directors
of the Collaborative

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.543
for Academic, Social
and Emotional Learning.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:50.790
So at this time, while we
are all in this pandemic

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.433
and worrying about our sense of isolation,

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.110
I think a lot of his work will resonate

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.940
and would be extremely
useful and enlightening

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.660
for all of us.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.270
He's the lead developer of RULER, sorry,

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.880
a systemic evidence-based
approach to social

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.410
and emotional learning
that has been adopted

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:17.410
by over 2,000 schools,
preschool through high schools

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.990
across the United States
and in many other countries,

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.660
including Australia,
China, England, Italy,

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.300
Mexico, and Spain.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.050
So what is really interesting about RULER

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:29.850
is that it infuses social
and emotional learning

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:33.780
into what he refers to as
the immune system of schools

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.350
by enhancing how school
administrators lead,

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.050
how educators teach, students
learn and families parent.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:45.840
So taking care of the full
ecosystem of integrating

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.470
this very important kind of intelligence

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:54.470
that has not been recognized
sufficiently or at least

00:03:54.726 --> 00:03:59.190
as important as it actually is.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.460
And research has shown
that this RULER boosts

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.310
academic performance,
decrease the school problems

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:08.230
like bullying, enriches classroom climates

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.513
and reduces teacher stress and burnout.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.930
So before further, you
know, I could go on and on,

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:17.500
Marc is extremely distinguished.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:21.330
The work that he has done
is so relevant and so timely

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:24.060
and I am so enthusiastic
about it that, you know,

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.950
I could go on and on but today,

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:31.220
he's gonna be talking
to us on his recent book

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.660
called "Permission to Feel:
Unlocking the Power of Emotions

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.630
to Help Our Kids,
Ourselves and Our Society",

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.933
which was released just
a couple of years ago.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.940
And before I turn it over to him,

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.850
I just wanted to mention a
couple of upcoming talks.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:52.580
We have the physicist and
philosopher, Carlo Rovelli

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:57.580
speaking on February 26th and
27th as part of a new series

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.900
that we are starting on the nature,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.810
understanding the nature of inference.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:06.160
So this is a new project that I've started

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.010
as part of the Frankie program,

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:13.023
a multidisciplinary exploration
of the nature of inference.

00:05:14.522 --> 00:05:18.130
And then we have on March
1st, Susan Hockfield,

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:23.130
who will give us a
distinguished speaker talk

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:25.140
at 4:00 p.m. as well.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.780
And as you all know, Susan Hockfield

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:31.780
is a former provost of Yale
and emeritus president of MIT.

00:05:33.487 --> 00:05:37.010
So please keep an eye out for our mailings

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:41.340
and join our mailing list if
you'd like personal information

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:46.340
about events and just to
close, I wanted to mention

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:51.720
that our ongoing learning by
doing project is something that

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.880
to solicit simple science
experiments for kids to do.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:00.500
So I invite all of you to
consider submitting ideas for this

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.650
and with that, I would like
to turn it over to Marc.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:07.560
Marc, welcome and thank you so much

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:11.180
for agreeing to give this talk.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.180
- Thank you, Priya, it's
a pleasure to be here.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.260
Welcome everybody.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.080
It's 4:15 Eastern time on a Thursday

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.830
and you're all signed up to
hear some guy from Connecticut

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.493
talk about feelings, I think
that's a good thing, right?

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:29.797
What do you think, Priya?

00:06:29.797 --> 00:06:32.820
- I think it's a fantastic
thing, it also tells you--

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.690
- Before everybody logged
in, you were sharing

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:38.740
like some big news that was
actually kind of fascinating.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:41.260
Do you mind just sharing
a little bit about that?

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:46.220
- Sure, so NASA just
landed a rover successfully

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:48.520
on the surface of Mars,
it's called Perseverance

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.950
and it was sort of a complicated maneuver

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:56.770
to actually land it and so
it's been a great success,

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:58.360
it is happening in real time.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.380
We have not yet gotten actual images

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:02.890
but we know that the landing was a success

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.120
and you know, this is a
very difficult, you know,

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.160
Mars has been very, very difficult.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.960
Many countries have
tried to land and failed

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.767
including India a few years ago.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.810
- Awesome, well, I was saying
to Priya and team like,

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:21.340
I don't know if I can beat that
one presentation today but--

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.970
- I don't think there's a competition

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:25.720
between the inner world and
the outer world, as I said,

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.920
they are both equally important.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:29.820
- I appreciate that, thank you.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.220
I just need to be able to use my screen.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:32.920
Someone, can you enable me please?

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.193
Awesome, thank you.

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:46.749
Can you see?
- Yes.

00:07:46.749 --> 00:07:48.287
- [Marc] Do you see the screen?

00:07:48.287 --> 00:07:49.740
- Yeah.
- Great.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.980
So thank you for the
introduction, Priya and team.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.900
I wanted to just start off by saying that

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.110
emotional intelligence has a special place

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:59.100
at Yale University.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.710
Some of you know that Peter Salovey,

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:04.820
the President of Yale was
the founder of the theory

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:07.670
of emotional intelligence back in 1990.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:12.327
He and my doctoral advisor,
Jack Mayer wrote a theory

00:08:12.327 --> 00:08:16.140
that no one really heard of
because when two academics write

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.310
a scholarly article, not a lot
of people in the real world

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.720
read it but I was one of the people

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:23.020
who got a chance to read it.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.500
And so were some other people

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:29.693
and the term got quite
popularized in the mid 1990s.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:35.220
And once that happened,
the field really took off

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:38.290
in terms of measurement,
in terms of training,

00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:41.410
in terms of thinking about
its application to schools

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:43.260
and homes and workplaces.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.920
And so what I'm gonna share
with you today is a little bit

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.340
about the work that we do at the Center

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:50.700
for Emotional Intelligence
that primarily focuses

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:53.100
on education context.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.400
And so I'm excited to share that with you

00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:00.230
and I'm gonna focus specifically,
Priya invited me to talk

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.300
about my book called "Permission to Feel"

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:06.230
which is essentially a
summary of the 25 years

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:10.690
of my running around the United
States and other countries,

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:14.160
trying to understand why
people are a little bit

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:16.410
reluctant to talk about their feelings

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:18.070
and what are the skills

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:20.880
that help us to use our emotions wisely.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:22.483
So here we go.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:28.850
I like to start with quotes
and I think that many of us

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.820
have been through multiple pandemics.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:34.470
We've experienced the coronavirus

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:37.580
for the last almost a year.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:41.950
We've seen fires burning homes

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:44.053
in California and other places.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:49.130
We have been made more
aware of systemic racism,

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:54.130
both here at our own university,
in this country, worldwide

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:58.180
and the list goes on, I
think in terms of the traumas

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:01.070
that many people have experienced

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:03.610
and I call myself a chronic

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.460
kind of slight neurotic optimist

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:10.647
and so I chose this quote for today.

00:10:10.647 --> 00:10:13.127
"And once the storm is over,

00:10:13.127 --> 00:10:15.627
"you won't remember how
you made it through,

00:10:15.627 --> 00:10:19.017
"how you managed to survive.

00:10:19.017 --> 00:10:21.847
"You wouldn't be sure whether
the storm is really over

00:10:21.847 --> 00:10:23.787
"but one thing is certain.

00:10:23.787 --> 00:10:25.627
"When you come out of the storm,

00:10:25.627 --> 00:10:27.997
"you won't be the same
person who walked in."

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.750
And that is by Haruki Murakami.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:35.690
So I wanna make this interactive and oops

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:38.700
and what I'd love to do is
just get people to share.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.550
When you think about that
quote, when you see that quote,

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:44.763
what does it make you think about?

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:48.670
If you can all just get
on your interaction hats,

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:49.670
that would be great.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:56.220
- [Priya] Yes, please feel
free to type in the chat

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:57.873
and we can read that out.

00:11:03.546 --> 00:11:07.310
So there's a comment from Simone who says,

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:09.753
I can get to really be present.

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:15.123
Makes her aware of the present.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.540
We have a request to put
the quote back up again.

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:22.190
- Yes, of course I thought
it was still there, sorry.

00:11:26.053 --> 00:11:28.750
- [Priya] Elizabeth
points out that we become

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:31.170
fundamentally changed by our experiences.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.823
And Chris refers to the
suffering in Texas right now.

00:11:35.961 --> 00:11:40.080
And Heda talks about
learning more about herself,

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:41.833
strengths and weaknesses.

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:48.190
And John Donatich mentions
that the belief that the self

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.423
is adaptable and ever-changing.

00:11:51.522 --> 00:11:54.903
A couple of people have
echoed that feeling.

00:11:55.830 --> 00:11:57.600
- Thank you, Priya.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.360
One of the things that I
wanna share that I learned

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:03.243
over the last 10 months
is around gratitude.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:05.700
Oftentimes, we think of gratitude,

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:07.580
like it's a big cliche these days, right?

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:09.710
We're told to write a gratitude journal.

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:12.620
We're told you should be grateful

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:16.850
and certainly, it's important.

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.190
But what I was surprised
by in a study that I ran

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:21.350
over the summer, I did
this study by myself.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:23.100
I was just really curious and I ran it

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.210
with about 1,000 participants
and I asked people

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:27.640
across the United States
to describe the things

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:29.400
for which they're grateful.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.660
And what we found in this
study was that people said

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:36.660
my family, my finances, my
security, that I have food,

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:40.460
all kind of the basics
that you might think of.

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.510
But one thing that stood out
to me as not being present

00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:48.163
on people's lists was things
like grocery store clerks,

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:52.480
the nurses and doctors that are
putting their lives at risk,

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:56.840
the teachers who are suffering
in terms of working from home

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:00.810
or go to school and
put themselves at risk.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:04.420
And so for me, one of my
takeaways from the pandemic

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:06.730
is just about gratitude.

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:09.520
I go now grocery shopping and
I see a nurse or a doctor,

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:10.760
when I see a first responder,

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:13.380
when I see someone who's
delivering a package

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:15.510
I just think about, my goodness.

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:17.530
I have the privilege to work from home

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:21.393
and I have that safety,
whereas many people don't.

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:24.330
And I think all of us
have learned something

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:25.820
from this pandemic, for sure.

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:29.263
That was just my one takeaway.

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:33.100
Now, this talk is about
emotional intelligence

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:36.760
and I would be doing a weird job

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:38.970
if I didn't ask you how you were feeling.

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:40.960
So at the Center for Emotional
Intelligence, we use a tool

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:44.340
we call The Mood Meter
and it's based in science

00:13:44.340 --> 00:13:47.400
that essentially asserts
that how we feel is a product

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:50.540
of two core dimensions
of what's called affect,

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:55.540
our appraisal and our arousal
or our activation levels.

00:13:56.690 --> 00:13:58.750
So on the x-axis, it asks you to think

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.040
about your pleasantness right now.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:04.250
From minus five, like this
is the worst experience

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:08.780
of my life to plus five, oh my
goodness, I'm taking an hour

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:12.160
out of my day to just sit
and think about emotion.

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:13.740
So just take a moment and think about

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.333
where you're at unpleasantness.

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:20.080
On the y-axis, it says the word energy,

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:22.790
literally think about
your physiology right now.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:26.070
Do you feel like your body is well rested

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:28.350
and that you've got lots of energy

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:29.960
or do you feel like you
just wanna jump at the bed

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:31.970
and pull the covers over your head?

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:33.990
So what's your body telling you right now,

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:35.243
energized or depleted?

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:39.079
Then we cross our two axes to create our,

00:14:39.079 --> 00:14:41.400
(speaking in foreign
language), our mood meter.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:43.130
Yellow, red, blue and green.

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:46.670
So take a moment and think about
what color you're in today.

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:48.070
Can everyone please do that?

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:52.960
Let's go into our chat
box and what's the color

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:55.503
that represents your
current emotional state?

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:02.170
Yellows, green.

00:15:02.170 --> 00:15:03.863
- [Priya] We have a lot of yellows.

00:15:06.932 --> 00:15:08.950
- Ty, you can share it if you like.

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:11.280
- [Ty] Yeah sure, we see a
lot of yellows, some greens.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:13.793
We have a blue, orange, yellow.

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:22.110
Priya is a yellow (chuckles)
and a bunch more greens.

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:24.200
Mostly green and yellow, I'd say.

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:25.033
- Cool.

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:29.420
So maybe this is like a special day

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:30.570
'cause the research that I've done

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:33.150
over the last couple of
months would not indicate

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:35.770
that there are a lot of
people in the yellow and green

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:39.230
but I'm gonna take what I can get today.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:42.510
What I'd like you to do right
now is convert your color

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:44.503
to a specific feeling word.

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:47.570
What would be the exact
word that you would use

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:49.510
to describe how you're feeling right now?

00:15:49.510 --> 00:15:51.640
Ready? You have three
seconds to write that word.

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:53.750
Three, two, one, (snaps).

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:58.070
Curious, relaxed, content,
peaceful, tired, thrilled.

00:15:58.070 --> 00:15:59.790
Okay, mellow, hopeful.

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:04.790
Okay, silly, irritated, curious, hopeful.

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:16.120
Just give me a yes or no
in the chat box quickly.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:18.650
How many of you found
it a little difficult

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:23.093
to go from the broad quadrant
to like that specific word?

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:32.370
So mostly yeses, any
hypotheses around that?

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:37.320
I mean, what might be the
reason that it is moderately

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:42.320
difficult to articulate,
words are not specific enough,

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.833
cognitive dissonance,
feeling more than one thing,

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:48.923
not easy to name our feelings.

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:54.850
Not used to practicing
it, lack of vocabulary.

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:57.600
There are a few words in squares.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:16:59.740
Nico, there are over 2,000 words

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:01.590
actually on a new study that we ran.

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:04.950
We found 16,000 words, feeling words

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:06.060
but most of us use what?

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:08.963
Two, fine, okay, whatever.

00:17:14.110 --> 00:17:16.290
Here's your cheat sheet by the way.

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:19.470
So this is an inside cover
of my buckets, the Mood Meter

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:24.470
with the words, we gave you
the first 100 as a practice,

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:27.483
to just build that vocabulary,
that basic vocabulary.

00:17:28.370 --> 00:17:31.780
And you can see here, there are
nuances in terms of building

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:35.283
that self-awareness,
that emotion language.

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:39.490
Now what I'd like you to do
for the next minute or so

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:41.740
is take a moment and
think about the strategy

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:44.640
that you think is gonna best serve you

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:45.950
to get the most out of today.

00:17:45.950 --> 00:17:48.810
So many of you said you
were in the yellow and green

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:52.340
and my question for you is
what might be the strategy

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:54.180
that would help you stay there?

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:57.150
Because probably being in the deep red,

00:17:57.150 --> 00:18:00.540
if you were enraged or
having feelings of panic,

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:03.540
you probably would have
difficulty concentrating.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.470
If you were in the deep
blue, you might be worrying

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:10.470
or thinking about the support
you need which is okay

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:13.073
but not the best for learning.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:16.323
So what might be your strategy?

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:21.270
Where does curious learner mode live?

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:23.750
I think that's kind of
motivated, focused, engaged.

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:25.000
What do you think, Laura?

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:31.423
You're gonna be open, pay attention.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:39.630
How many of you, if you have children,

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:43.280
who've had like complete kind of meltdowns

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:44.743
during the pandemic.

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:48.880
When your kid is having a
meltdown, you say, come on honey,

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:52.453
just be open or come
on, just pay attention?

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:57.110
Crystal is away from the kids.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:18:59.700
So that's your strategy, is
giving yourself some space

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:00.650
away from the kids.

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:03.253
Other strategies?

00:19:05.960 --> 00:19:08.790
Breathe, listen, same thing.

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:11.120
Are you gonna just tell
your kids, just breathe,

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:11.973
just listen?

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:14.683
Happiness is a choice.

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:19.333
Focus.

00:19:20.962 --> 00:19:23.610
It's tough, let me get a raise of hands.

00:19:23.610 --> 00:19:25.870
Everyone know how to
raise their hands in Zoom?

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:29.130
Give me a quick raise of hands
if over the last nine months,

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:31.290
you have struggled at
some point or another

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:33.143
with regulating your own feelings.

00:19:36.850 --> 00:19:41.450
Okay, a lot of people, let
me ask you another question.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:45.120
How many of you have struggled,
raise your hands again,

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:49.523
with dealing with other people's feelings?

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:58.213
Okay, so we're all in the
same boat pretty much.

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:03.140
What our research shows at
the Center is the following

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.160
that a lot of people are
experiencing some pretty

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:07.280
intense emotions right now.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:08.970
From over the last nine months,

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:11.680
I think we studied well over 30,000 people

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:14.960
and what we found continuously is anxiety

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:18.320
is the number one emotion
that people report feeling.

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:20.120
When we asked them, well,
how do you wanna feel?

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:23.570
We find very different emotions
during different times.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:24.940
So over the summer, for example,

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:26.920
we asked a couple thousand
teachers and they said,

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:29.610
we wanna be excited and then
we asked them in the fall

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:31.060
and they said, no,
forget about excitement.

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:34.163
We just want to be appreciated
and valued and supported.

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:38.540
And so what we have here
are interesting phenomenon.

00:20:38.540 --> 00:20:43.170
We've got our emotion state,
the one that we're feeling

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:45.930
and then we have our, what
we might call ideal effect

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:48.740
or our hope for emotion state.

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:51.683
And oftentimes, there's a big
discrepancy between the two.

00:20:55.170 --> 00:20:58.750
And so that leads us to think
about what's the goal here?

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:01.320
Is the goal to go from that red and blue

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.630
and just be in the yellow and green?

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:04.530
What do you think?

00:21:05.740 --> 00:21:07.260
I would ask you, what is the goal?

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:10.653
Like what are we supposed to
do with all of our feelings?

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:12.820
What would you say?

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:20.800
What would you say?

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:26.880
Share them, be patient, accept them.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:29.983
Don't get stuck in any one
feeling, share in order to grow.

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:33.530
Recognize and be aware,
understand and accept them.

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:37.903
I think all of you are
doing pretty well here.

00:21:38.830 --> 00:21:43.023
Strife happens but remember
the weather changes, okay.

00:21:44.735 --> 00:21:46.870
Well, one thing that I wanna get to today

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:50.770
is that there's a
difficult challenge we have

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:53.123
with this idea of striving for happiness.

00:21:53.963 --> 00:21:57.350
You know, the founding
documents of our country

00:21:57.350 --> 00:22:00.993
or the report that we
have to pursue happiness.

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.810
A colleague who was here
at Yale, June Gruber

00:22:04.810 --> 00:22:07.630
who we used to do some research together

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:11.550
did some research on the
dark side of happiness.

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:15.830
That when we strive daily,
like we have this kind of goal

00:22:15.830 --> 00:22:17.440
to be happy all the time,

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:19.563
it can lead to a lot of disappointment.

00:22:21.360 --> 00:22:23.410
Think about that, you're
bound to be disappointed

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:24.860
if every day you're trying to be happy.

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:27.950
I don't know about you but
like, that's really hard for me.

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.460
Anyone relate to that?

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:32.963
That this striving for
happiness can actually backfire.

00:22:33.920 --> 00:22:34.870
Does that resonate?

00:22:37.690 --> 00:22:41.173
And so maybe we need to think
about it a little differently.

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:46.210
Actually I just did an hour
and a half session today

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:49.170
with this group on toxic positivity,

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:50.910
this idea of like always having to show up

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.830
being happy and odd.

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:55.820
And you can imagine when
you're a boss or a parent

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:57.990
or a teacher always like demonstrating

00:22:57.990 --> 00:22:59.960
that you're in a good
mood or feeling optimistic

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:04.960
and positive, how that might
just rub people the wrong way.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:07.620
But it also, what does
that do for the children

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:09.000
that we are raising and teaching?

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:11.600
It makes them think like the
only emotion that matters

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:15.433
is happiness which probably
is not what we want.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:19.270
So the vision of the Center
for Emotional Intelligence

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:21.460
is to use the power of
emotions to create a healthier

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:23.760
and more innovative and
compassionate society.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:29.920
I argue in my most recent book
that it starts with ourselves

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:35.860
and giving everyone else this
concept of permission to feel

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:38.130
and I think a lot of you
actually, when I asked you

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:42.343
about what the goal is, you
intuitively got to this point.

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:45.363
So here I am asking you right now.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:51.400
In whatever role you have
in life, a student, a mom

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:55.350
a dad, a professor, multiple,
you can be all of those roles,

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:58.673
when you think about the
concept of permission to feel,

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:01.630
what does it make you think about?

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.193
And just take a moment
and jot down your ideas.

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:19.023
Vulnerability, freewill,

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:25.310
permission to stop failing
sometimes, not being lawyerly.

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:27.930
I actually did a talk for the
general counsel's office here

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:30.100
years ago and one lawyer said to me,

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:31.583
there's no feelings in law.

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:37.023
Let go of our emotions, being human,

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:40.180
no need to have to explain
yourself all the time.

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:41.083
Vulnerability.

00:24:44.460 --> 00:24:48.600
How many of you feel
that in your current role

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:53.600
that you have the freedom to be your true,

00:24:53.860 --> 00:24:54.980
full feeling self?

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:57.650
Or how many of you feel
like there are times

00:24:57.650 --> 00:25:00.950
when actually you need to kind of fake it

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:05.393
or you can't actually
express your honest feelings.

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:10.430
So many of us, right?

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:14.540
Because these concepts don't live

00:25:14.540 --> 00:25:16.630
only within us as individuals.

00:25:16.630 --> 00:25:20.700
These are concepts that
live in homes and schools

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:23.300
and workplaces, in communities.

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:26.470
And so one of the other things
that we have to think about

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:30.170
is do we have a permission
to feel culture and climate

00:25:30.170 --> 00:25:32.163
in our homes, schools and workplaces?

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:38.590
Now I've asked a lot of people
and you're right, Clara,

00:25:38.590 --> 00:25:41.570
it depends on the
situation and the emotion.

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:45.520
So over the last year
I've done, I don't know,

00:25:45.520 --> 00:25:47.900
a couple hundred presentations
in a couple of years.

00:25:47.900 --> 00:25:50.180
And I've asked literally
tens of thousands of people

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:51.530
and have compiled the data,

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:55.040
when you think of this permission to feel,

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.020
what are the characteristics?

00:25:57.020 --> 00:26:00.470
And what we find is that people
say compassionate, empathic,

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:03.880
nonjudgmental, supportive,
unconditional love,

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:05.063
patient, supportive.

00:26:06.980 --> 00:26:11.560
So I wonder A, how many
of you are these things

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:12.793
for yourself right now?

00:26:14.060 --> 00:26:18.990
Self-compassionate, being
vulnerable even with yourself,

00:26:18.990 --> 00:26:21.160
showing self-care and empathy,

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:24.020
being nonjudgmental of your own feelings.

00:26:24.020 --> 00:26:25.460
Or how many of you feel like, you know,

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:27.713
sometimes I could use
some work in this area?

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:30.700
Anyone feel like they could do some work?

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:36.100
And how about with other people?

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:41.360
Especially when you're
experiencing strong,

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:42.893
unpleasant feelings.

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:46.332
It's hard to be compassionate,
empathic, nonjudgmental,

00:26:46.332 --> 00:26:48.153
supportive when you're
activated, isn't it?

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:57.180
Now, what I wanna jump to
is the science around this.

00:26:57.180 --> 00:26:59.830
So we know from interviewing people

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:01.430
that there are multiple components

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:03.870
of giving oneself permission to feel

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:07.860
but we're at Yale university
and I've done my share

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:10.110
of presentations in different departments.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:13.080
And I must say that some
people, you know, they're like,

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:16.510
Marc, we like your stories,
we like the anecdotes

00:27:16.510 --> 00:27:19.670
but what's the science?

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:22.680
And as Priya told you,

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:24.060
we don't just talk about these things.

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:27.240
We do rigorous research and
I oftentimes tell people

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:30.070
and I think this is the
danger in a lot of our fields,

00:27:30.070 --> 00:27:32.390
especially in applied psychology

00:27:32.390 --> 00:27:33.640
is that people always ask me,

00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:36.020
so Marc, what's your
opinion about something?

00:27:36.020 --> 00:27:41.020
And I always tell people like,
I'm fine sharing my opinion

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:44.550
but what you really wanna
know is what is the science

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:46.980
behind what I'm speaking about?

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:49.430
So when I talk about emotion regulation,

00:27:49.430 --> 00:27:51.600
it's not what matters is my opinion.

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:54.140
What matters is what the data
show or the science shows

00:27:54.140 --> 00:27:56.437
in terms of what works for
people and supporting them

00:27:56.437 --> 00:27:58.650
and managing their feelings.

00:27:58.650 --> 00:28:00.770
And so I think what's most
important for all of you

00:28:00.770 --> 00:28:04.020
to understand is A, we should be blessed

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:08.180
that we have a president
at Yale who took all of the

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:11.621
kind of science from the
1930s and '40s and '50s

00:28:11.621 --> 00:28:14.120
and '60s and '70s and '80s and compiled it

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:17.310
into a comprehensive
theory and made it public

00:28:17.310 --> 00:28:19.640
that there is a way to be
smart about our feelings.

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:22.493
That emotions are not
disruptive, they're information.

00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:27.610
And that they matter for five
really important reasons.

00:28:27.610 --> 00:28:30.120
The first is attention,
memory and learning.

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:31.670
Think about it.

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:34.640
If you're bored right
now with my presentation,

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:35.730
you're doing something else.

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:38.140
You're not paying attention
because your emotions

00:28:38.140 --> 00:28:40.640
are driving your attentional capacity.

00:28:40.640 --> 00:28:42.410
My friend, Mary Helen Immordino-Yang,

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:43.960
she's a neuroscientist, she goes,

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:46.853
we learn what we care about.

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:53.100
Which means that this is
also a relational experience.

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:55.860
Like I am in many ways co-regulating

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:57.473
your experience right now.

00:28:57.473 --> 00:29:00.520
Because if I talk like this for an hour

00:29:00.520 --> 00:29:03.650
and I just said emotions matter
and I just had a really like

00:29:03.650 --> 00:29:07.810
kind of bored facial expression
and really monotone voice.

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:09.310
How many of you would log off?

00:29:10.275 --> 00:29:12.490
(participant laughing)

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:15.280
Somebody's laughing
behind the scenes, right?

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:17.600
You'd be like, you may
stay on out of respect

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:20.120
for whatever bizarre reason
but you would be like,

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:22.820
Oh my goodness, I can take
that, I'm gonna lose it.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:25.670
Has anyone ever had a class
like that where you're like,

00:29:25.670 --> 00:29:28.840
the content is something
that you're passionate about

00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:33.570
but the presentation is like,
has anyone ever had that?

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:35.373
It's like, it's painful, isn't it?

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:40.590
And so what this tells us
is that emotions are what?

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:43.200
They're relational experiences.

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:48.200
Decision-making, how we feel
influences the choices we make.

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:50.240
Relationship quality.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:52.110
We say in our Center
that emotions are signals

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.230
to approach or avoid.

00:29:54.230 --> 00:29:56.020
Has anyone here ever worked with someone

00:29:56.020 --> 00:29:58.220
or lived with someone
who is very difficult?

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:01.410
Like just difficult to be with?

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:05.850
And how many of you, when
you worked for that person

00:30:05.850 --> 00:30:08.160
or lived with that person
would say to yourself,

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:10.963
gosh, I can't wait to spend
more time with them today.

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:15.990
No, you wake up in the
morning and you're like ooh,

00:30:15.990 --> 00:30:18.127
I can't believe I have to deal with this.

00:30:18.127 --> 00:30:19.960
Oh, do I have to say hello?

00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:20.847
Or at work, you look at
them and you're like,

00:30:20.847 --> 00:30:23.030
I'm gonna go the other way.

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:28.030
Because again, think about
this co-regulatory process.

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:29.990
When you look at other
people's facial expressions,

00:30:29.990 --> 00:30:32.240
we have memories of the
way they've treated us

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:34.440
and they create activation
levels within us.

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:37.140
Physical and mental health.

00:30:39.020 --> 00:30:43.300
One of the studies we did showed
that literally how we feel

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:45.350
and kind of in terms of
the culture and climate

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:47.950
of our organization influences
our own stress levels,

00:30:47.950 --> 00:30:51.850
which influences our physical
health, like sleep problems,

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:56.260
our body mass index, which
is an indicator of body fat.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:59.000
And then finally
performance in creativity.

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:01.430
One of our scientists, her name is Zorana.

00:31:01.430 --> 00:31:03.850
She studies the link between
emotions and creativity

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:08.540
and she says, emotions
are the fuel to creativity

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:12.590
but it's our emotional
intelligence that helps us

00:31:12.590 --> 00:31:14.853
make that creative
process an actual product.

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:17.950
And this always reminds me
of being a professor here

00:31:17.950 --> 00:31:21.200
at Yale, where all of my
students have like amazing grade

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:25.290
point averages and they
all have high test scores,

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:28.400
SAT scores, GREs for graduate school.

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:31.810
And many would think, well,
okay, if academic ability

00:31:31.810 --> 00:31:35.200
is the driver of success, then
everyone here should end up

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:40.200
being highly successful
because the range is so tight

00:31:40.290 --> 00:31:42.850
but it's the exact reason
why that doesn't matter.

00:31:42.850 --> 00:31:46.310
When you have a restriction
of range of a construct,

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:49.390
it can no longer predict outcome.

00:31:49.390 --> 00:31:53.090
So like basketball, everyone
who is on a basketball team.

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:55.320
I mean, from what I gather is really tall.

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:59.080
Like, I don't think I'm 5'8
and like eight and a quarter.

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:02.080
So like they're not picking me
to be on any basketball team.

00:32:03.100 --> 00:32:06.260
I can't even, you know, I'm
not gonna get into that,

00:32:06.260 --> 00:32:10.080
so height no longer really is a predictor

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:12.400
of success in basketball.

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:16.423
Just like at Yale, IQ,
great GPA, test scores

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:21.460
is no longer a predictive variable.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:23.230
Does this resonate with people?

00:32:23.230 --> 00:32:26.720
That means there has to be
other things, other attributes

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:30.250
of who we are that
contribute to our success

00:32:30.250 --> 00:32:33.600
and what I'm arguing of course
and have the evidence for

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:36.450
is that a big part of that is
our emotional intelligence.

00:32:37.650 --> 00:32:41.270
So my question for all of
you is do you see yourself

00:32:41.270 --> 00:32:43.573
as an emotion scientist
or an emotion judge?

00:32:46.210 --> 00:32:47.210
Are you curious?

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:48.343
Are you an explorer?

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:50.650
Are you reflective?

00:32:50.650 --> 00:32:54.550
Do you want me to get specific
or do you see yourself

00:32:54.550 --> 00:32:56.163
as more of like, you know what?

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:01.090
I don't really talk about
feelings, they get in the way.

00:33:01.090 --> 00:33:03.890
I had a teacher last
year, say to me, you know,

00:33:03.890 --> 00:33:05.843
I check my emotions at the door.

00:33:07.231 --> 00:33:10.800
I have to just put them in a
box and leave them outside.

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:13.810
How many of you feel like
you can check your emotions

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:16.710
at the door before you go home,

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:19.330
when you just like go to work really?

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:21.720
Or when you're having a difficult morning,

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:24.470
how many of you like, I'm
just gonna check my emotions?

00:33:25.500 --> 00:33:28.300
And I'd leave them in my car
before I go into my office?

00:33:29.476 --> 00:33:31.076
It doesn't really work that way.

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:35.270
'Cause our brains don't operate,

00:33:35.270 --> 00:33:37.173
we can't pull out the emotion center.

00:33:38.030 --> 00:33:39.980
It just doesn't work that way.

00:33:39.980 --> 00:33:42.870
When you fail at dealing
with your feelings,

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:46.273
like many of you say you have,
what do you say to yourself?

00:33:47.350 --> 00:33:49.650
Let me ask you that right now, be honest,

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:53.060
when you like really mess
up and you say something

00:33:53.060 --> 00:33:56.260
that you regret or you eat
something you don't wanna eat,

00:33:56.260 --> 00:34:01.260
or you just engage in a behavior

00:34:01.780 --> 00:34:04.010
that you didn't want to engage
in because you were upset,

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:07.970
you yelled, you scream, you ignored.

00:34:07.970 --> 00:34:11.160
What is the dialogue that
you have with yourself?

00:34:11.160 --> 00:34:15.733
I'm a bad person, I'm
so stupid, I screwed up.

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:20.460
I'm so much of a fixed mindset, right?

00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:21.883
That emotion judge, right?

00:34:23.240 --> 00:34:25.650
And so one of the things
that we try to help people

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:28.670
with is understand A, our
emotions are a femoral

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:32.600
and B, that we are continuous learners

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:35.300
and that's more of a
growth mindset around it.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:39.670
You fail, it's like, okay, I
can apologize, I can forgive.

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:42.060
I can learn more and I
can do better next time

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:45.780
and so that's basically getting at

00:34:47.270 --> 00:34:48.580
that these are hard skills.

00:34:48.580 --> 00:34:52.503
Oftentimes, emotions are
thought about as soft skills.

00:34:53.410 --> 00:34:54.780
Does that resonate with you?

00:34:54.780 --> 00:34:58.220
People think of it as soft
science or soft skills

00:34:58.220 --> 00:35:00.810
which by the way, it makes me
crazy since my whole career

00:35:00.810 --> 00:35:04.020
is as an emotion scientist
but nevertheless,

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:07.760
that's the mindset and
I always joke around

00:35:07.760 --> 00:35:10.170
having been at Yale now for
20 years, has anyone here

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:11.900
at Yale ever met someone
who was really smart

00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:13.303
but not emotionally skilled?

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:17.683
Please don't write that
person's name in the chat.

00:35:19.940 --> 00:35:23.710
So that leads us to bring in the skills.

00:35:23.710 --> 00:35:25.663
So what are the underlying skills?

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:28.830
R-U-L-E-R is the acronym that we use.

00:35:28.830 --> 00:35:32.010
Recognizing emotions in self and others.

00:35:32.010 --> 00:35:35.260
Understanding the causes and consequences.

00:35:35.260 --> 00:35:37.750
Labeling emotions
accurately, having the words,

00:35:37.750 --> 00:35:40.210
knowing how and when to
express our feelings.

00:35:40.210 --> 00:35:42.373
Having the strategies to regulate them.

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:46.830
So self-awareness and other awareness.

00:35:46.830 --> 00:35:48.750
The ability to manage one's own emotions,

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:51.923
the ability to help
others in managing theirs.

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:55.980
So I did a talk a couple of times ago

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:57.510
for a bunch of leaders and I asked them,

00:35:57.510 --> 00:36:00.720
what's the psychological
difference between stressed,

00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:02.433
overwhelmed, anxious?

00:36:03.490 --> 00:36:05.840
Anyone wanna take a stab at that?

00:36:05.840 --> 00:36:09.423
Stressed, overwhelmed, anxious.

00:36:10.450 --> 00:36:11.573
What's the difference?

00:36:17.170 --> 00:36:19.113
Level of intensity, they're the same.

00:36:25.430 --> 00:36:28.030
Oh, under the judgment,
different way of expressing.

00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:31.550
So what I'm trying to get at with our work

00:36:31.550 --> 00:36:34.600
is the granularity in
terms of these differences.

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:36.790
So let me share what I mean.

00:36:36.790 --> 00:36:39.900
Stressed, when you're
stressed, think about it.

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:42.720
Stress has to do with
having a lot of demands

00:36:42.720 --> 00:36:45.110
but feeling like you just
don't have the resources

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:47.313
to fulfill those demands.

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:53.770
Anxiety is this perception that
there's a lot of uncertainty

00:36:53.770 --> 00:36:56.300
around the future, like
for example, vaccinations,

00:36:56.300 --> 00:36:57.630
like some of us are waiting.

00:36:57.630 --> 00:36:58.720
We don't know when it's gonna happen,

00:36:58.720 --> 00:36:59.930
it's uncertain to me around that.

00:36:59.930 --> 00:37:02.130
We don't know what the
new strain of the virus,

00:37:02.130 --> 00:37:04.940
is that gonna mess things up even more?

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:06.690
That's what puts us in anxiety

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:09.163
It's not stress, that's actual anxiety.

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:14.270
Overwhelmed is when you're
overcome by your emotions.

00:37:14.270 --> 00:37:15.860
When you just feel like you
have so much on your plate,

00:37:15.860 --> 00:37:18.880
you just can't breathe,
you can't take it anymore.

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:20.380
So people see the differences?

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:24.240
Does that resonate with you?

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:26.040
Now, the question is does it matter?

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:28.993
Does it matter?

00:37:28.993 --> 00:37:32.693
Would it matter for us to
be able to be granular?

00:37:34.320 --> 00:37:36.433
So I ask you why, why would that matter?

00:37:39.350 --> 00:37:41.047
Seriously, why does it matter?

00:37:50.401 --> 00:37:51.880
It helps us manage better.

00:37:51.880 --> 00:37:53.930
Yeah, there's a saying that we have,

00:37:53.930 --> 00:37:55.690
you have to name it to tame it.

00:37:55.690 --> 00:37:59.880
You gotta label it to regulate
it and think about it.

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:02.690
If you're working with someone
who you're looking at them

00:38:02.690 --> 00:38:04.710
in terms of their behavior.

00:38:04.710 --> 00:38:06.160
So let's just try this
right now very quick.

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:07.310
I'm gonna stop sharing.

00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:10.310
So imagine I come into your office

00:38:10.310 --> 00:38:11.696
I'm a colleague of yours and I'm like,

00:38:11.696 --> 00:38:13.447
I can't take it anymore.

00:38:13.447 --> 00:38:15.970
(Ty laughing)

00:38:15.970 --> 00:38:19.033
Okay, Ty, you're laughing
at me, thanks a lot.

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:22.700
All right, so how am I feeling?

00:38:28.199 --> 00:38:30.133
- [Priya] How are you feeling
if somebody comes in--?

00:38:30.133 --> 00:38:31.570
- No, no, how am I feeling?

00:38:31.570 --> 00:38:34.053
What is the feeling that I'm having?

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:37.030
I can't take it anymore.

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:37.980
- [Ty] Overwhelmed.

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:41.490
- So what happened just then in there?

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:44.573
Did you become the emotion
scientist or the emotion judge?

00:38:48.680 --> 00:38:50.373
And why did you become the judge?

00:38:52.820 --> 00:38:54.920
Because you attributed emotion to me.

00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:59.920
You made an assumption that
my behavior meant something

00:38:59.977 --> 00:39:04.210
in terms of anger, frustration,
whatever it might be

00:39:04.210 --> 00:39:07.150
and so it's a big deal in our work that

00:39:07.150 --> 00:39:08.940
in order to really know
how someone is feeling

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:11.190
A, you have to ask them and
B, they have to tell you

00:39:11.190 --> 00:39:12.590
and that means they have to be comfortable

00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:13.853
and feel safe to do so.

00:39:15.390 --> 00:39:20.390
And we oftentimes grossly make mistakes.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.620
We make big mistakes when
we're judging someone

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:24.780
by their behavior, we have stereotypes,

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:27.560
we have racial stereotypes,
we have gender stereotypes.

00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:30.850
We've got, I mean, endless
kinds of stereotypes,

00:39:30.850 --> 00:39:32.500
power stereotypes.

00:39:32.500 --> 00:39:37.500
And so when we are observing behavior

00:39:37.690 --> 00:39:40.600
and we activate stereotypic information,

00:39:40.600 --> 00:39:43.723
we're automatically biased in
our interpretation of others.

00:39:44.780 --> 00:39:46.480
Does this resonate with everybody?

00:39:48.550 --> 00:39:52.170
- [Priya] Yes but Marc, I had
a quick question if I may.

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:55.780
If part of the problem is
that we are not able to even

00:39:55.780 --> 00:39:59.710
personally articulate how
we are feeling as we saw.

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:03.070
I mean, isn't that like
the first fundamental layer

00:40:03.070 --> 00:40:04.700
of the problem that--?

00:40:04.700 --> 00:40:07.870
- It is and so you've
gotta have the language

00:40:07.870 --> 00:40:10.577
in order to describe feelings
'cause that's the only way

00:40:10.577 --> 00:40:12.463
we know how to communicate in this world.

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:17.030
And then you have to use that language,

00:40:17.030 --> 00:40:19.540
understand the reasons for
those different feelings,

00:40:19.540 --> 00:40:22.280
states like anger is around injustice

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:25.250
and disappointment is
around unmet expectations.

00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:27.340
Then you've gotta get a notice in others.

00:40:27.340 --> 00:40:30.560
You gotta confirm that that's
how they're actually feeling

00:40:30.560 --> 00:40:33.520
'cause you can't just, if
I start, I hate you, right?

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:34.760
I can't take it, you think I'm angry

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:36.700
but it could be that I'm feeling shame.

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:38.240
I can tell you in my childhood

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:41.040
because I had some serious
bullying in my childhood,

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:45.410
I would come home like angry,
displaying a lot of anger

00:40:45.410 --> 00:40:47.683
but it was feeling a
lot of shame and fear.

00:40:48.840 --> 00:40:51.930
And my parents constantly
misperceived me as being angry

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:54.400
and then I got in trouble
and then I got punished

00:40:54.400 --> 00:40:56.210
and no one really supported
me because I was actually

00:40:56.210 --> 00:41:00.143
feeling much deeper emotions like shame.

00:41:00.990 --> 00:41:04.820
So yes, it's self-awareness,
building the language,

00:41:04.820 --> 00:41:06.030
understanding the strategies

00:41:06.030 --> 00:41:08.658
and then building relationships,

00:41:08.658 --> 00:41:10.850
giving other people
that permission to feel,

00:41:10.850 --> 00:41:13.780
being that emotion scientist with them

00:41:13.780 --> 00:41:17.270
and then using that wisely.

00:41:17.270 --> 00:41:18.273
So good question, Priya.

00:41:18.273 --> 00:41:22.040
- [Priya] And then part of
this masquerading of, you know,

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:25.370
don't we often do that
even as a way to cope?

00:41:25.370 --> 00:41:28.890
Like we map something that
is, like you mentioned,

00:41:28.890 --> 00:41:33.560
in your example, you map
something that's much more intense

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:37.900
than a shame into something
else, which is anger, right?

00:41:37.900 --> 00:41:41.690
So doesn't that serve a
purpose for us personally?

00:41:41.690 --> 00:41:44.210
We do that intentionally, don't we?

00:41:44.210 --> 00:41:47.050
- Well, I think we do
it because as a society,

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:50.230
we have deemed that
shame is a weak emotion

00:41:51.169 --> 00:41:54.450
and so it's certainly
not beneficial, right?

00:41:54.450 --> 00:41:57.400
Because we're not gonna get
the supports that we need

00:41:57.400 --> 00:42:00.457
to manage the true feeling if
we mask it with another one.

00:42:02.070 --> 00:42:04.514
A lot of work we got to do here, Priya.

00:42:04.514 --> 00:42:06.340
- [Priya] Thank you very much, yeah.

00:42:06.340 --> 00:42:09.130
- So I just wanted to
share a couple more slides.

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:11.617
One is on a study that we
did on emotional intelligence

00:42:11.617 --> 00:42:14.183
and supervisor and that's a typo.

00:42:15.175 --> 00:42:16.900
This is a study where we looked at

00:42:16.900 --> 00:42:19.680
supervisor emotional
intelligence, high and low

00:42:20.650 --> 00:42:23.780
and then different aspects of employees.

00:42:23.780 --> 00:42:26.510
So skill acquisition, people
feel like they are more likely

00:42:26.510 --> 00:42:28.480
to acquire skills when
they have a supervisor

00:42:28.480 --> 00:42:30.430
who has higher and emotional intelligence,

00:42:30.430 --> 00:42:31.870
much fewer turnover and tensions,

00:42:31.870 --> 00:42:34.820
much greater job
satisfaction, less burnout,

00:42:34.820 --> 00:42:39.500
slightly more engagement,
less fearful of speaking up.

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:44.500
There was actual more ethics
in a culture in an organization

00:42:44.690 --> 00:42:47.543
with a supervisor with higher
emotional intelligence.

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:50.760
So the last thing I'll
share with you today

00:42:50.760 --> 00:42:52.020
and then we can take some questions

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:55.410
is that right now during
the pandemic especially,

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:58.880
people have been asking
us what are the strategies

00:42:58.880 --> 00:43:01.190
to take care of ourselves, to
take care about the people?

00:43:01.190 --> 00:43:02.420
And I'll just end by saying,

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:05.673
the first is this concept
of permission to feel.

00:43:06.660 --> 00:43:09.290
Then we've got to help ourselves
regulate those feelings.

00:43:09.290 --> 00:43:11.083
We've gotta maybe take that breath.

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.140
We also have to do some physical self-care

00:43:15.140 --> 00:43:16.930
like making sure that we get enough asleep

00:43:16.930 --> 00:43:21.600
and eat nutritious foods and
move as much as possible.

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:23.410
We have the psychological self-care,

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:26.950
taking time to be still and
just doing some fun things.

00:43:26.950 --> 00:43:29.370
Social connection, we know
is a big one right now

00:43:29.370 --> 00:43:32.170
because we have a pandemic
of loneliness as well.

00:43:32.170 --> 00:43:34.140
So how do you remain
connected in this strange,

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:36.200
socially distant world?

00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:37.820
Cognitive strategies.

00:43:37.820 --> 00:43:41.503
The big one that we focus
on in our work is self-talk.

00:43:42.530 --> 00:43:44.500
How many of us have
that negative self-talk,

00:43:44.500 --> 00:43:47.900
that automatic habitual
way of being a self-critic

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:50.300
as opposed to someone who
is self compassionate?

00:43:51.460 --> 00:43:53.020
And then routines

00:43:53.020 --> 00:43:55.220
in terms of setting
yourself up for success.

00:43:56.430 --> 00:43:58.660
So I thought maybe we can
just do one little exercise

00:43:58.660 --> 00:44:01.840
and maybe we can just do a
little breathing exercise

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:04.240
to bring ourselves into
the present moment.

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:06.590
Let me just take a nice
long inhale and exhale.

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:17.220
Just bring ourselves in to
kind of digest this information

00:44:24.310 --> 00:44:26.860
and I'll just wrap up by
sharing a few last things.

00:44:27.740 --> 00:44:31.100
So RULER, you heard in my
intro is our center's approach

00:44:31.100 --> 00:44:33.930
to teaching social and emotional learning.

00:44:33.930 --> 00:44:36.290
Our goal is to get away
from piecemeal approaches

00:44:36.290 --> 00:44:40.270
to more systemic models and
we have a theory of change.

00:44:40.270 --> 00:44:42.450
And I just wanna say
that one of the things

00:44:42.450 --> 00:44:45.070
that we're trying to do in our work

00:44:45.070 --> 00:44:48.053
is get people away from
the quick fix mentality.

00:44:49.050 --> 00:44:52.113
So like here's your emotional
times workshop, good luck.

00:44:53.030 --> 00:44:54.690
This is hard work, I can tell you

00:44:54.690 --> 00:44:57.040
as someone who's studied this
and practiced it for 20 years

00:44:57.040 --> 00:44:59.633
and still 25 years, 30 years.

00:45:00.640 --> 00:45:03.680
We gotta move away from like
the one-size-fits-all thing,

00:45:03.680 --> 00:45:05.810
like mindfulness has
become very popular now.

00:45:05.810 --> 00:45:09.988
It's very positive and
useful but at the same time,

00:45:09.988 --> 00:45:12.010
some people don't benefit from that.

00:45:12.010 --> 00:45:13.373
They like other strategies.

00:45:15.230 --> 00:45:16.063
We have to think about this

00:45:16.063 --> 00:45:17.760
not from an individual's perspective

00:45:17.760 --> 00:45:21.460
but from our team at work,
our entire department

00:45:21.460 --> 00:45:24.050
and we're doing this at the
Child Study Center right now.

00:45:24.050 --> 00:45:26.650
We've had some challenges
over the last seven months

00:45:27.670 --> 00:45:32.170
in multiple domains from
people's experiences of racism

00:45:32.170 --> 00:45:37.170
to people feeling disconnected
and the only way to get

00:45:37.180 --> 00:45:38.860
through this is to take
a systemic approach.

00:45:38.860 --> 00:45:40.310
It's not about pulling someone out

00:45:40.310 --> 00:45:41.840
and giving them some training.

00:45:41.840 --> 00:45:44.150
It really is about shifting
the culture and climate

00:45:44.150 --> 00:45:47.160
of the organization and that
means that we have to think

00:45:47.160 --> 00:45:48.490
about this as a university.

00:45:48.490 --> 00:45:49.680
It means we have to think about this

00:45:49.680 --> 00:45:52.910
in terms of the policies
that govern our country.

00:45:52.910 --> 00:45:56.130
So for example, detaining
children at borders

00:45:56.130 --> 00:45:58.820
probably is not good for
their healthy development.

00:45:58.820 --> 00:46:01.500
So how do we inform policy makers, right?

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:05.393
About the principles of
healthy, emotional development?

00:46:06.410 --> 00:46:10.373
And so from the micro
level to the macro level.

00:46:12.010 --> 00:46:14.410
We have an app that we
call The Mood Meter App,

00:46:14.410 --> 00:46:15.890
very excited.

00:46:15.890 --> 00:46:18.160
One of our Yale graduates,
his name is Ben Silbermann

00:46:18.160 --> 00:46:20.930
who is the founder of Pinterest
is supporting our Center

00:46:20.930 --> 00:46:22.560
and the work and we're
building out a new app.

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:24.430
It's gonna be like, just incredible.

00:46:24.430 --> 00:46:27.090
So excited about it, it'll
have multiple features.

00:46:27.090 --> 00:46:28.590
This is our beta version.

00:46:28.590 --> 00:46:32.417
So if you're interested in
plotting your emotions over time,

00:46:32.417 --> 00:46:36.130
you can download that and let me wrap up

00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:37.700
and then we'll take just a few questions.

00:46:37.700 --> 00:46:41.080
First, you gotta evaluate your routines.

00:46:41.080 --> 00:46:42.683
Is your day working for you?

00:46:43.980 --> 00:46:45.323
Try to be a preventionist.

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:48.980
Surround yourself with
people who are calming.

00:46:48.980 --> 00:46:52.860
I can't tell you the power
of the presence of someone

00:46:52.860 --> 00:46:57.860
who can bring your
nervous system levels down

00:46:58.127 --> 00:47:00.453
and your sympathetic arousal down.

00:47:01.970 --> 00:47:03.780
Be aware of emotional contagion.

00:47:03.780 --> 00:47:05.870
It works for both pleasant
and unpleasant emotions.

00:47:05.870 --> 00:47:07.730
So a stressed out parent
is a stressed out home.

00:47:07.730 --> 00:47:11.490
A stressed out boss is
a stressed out workplace

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:14.350
and so we have to monitor those feelings.

00:47:14.350 --> 00:47:16.140
I say be an uncle Marvin,

00:47:16.140 --> 00:47:18.340
that was the person who
was my hero in life.

00:47:19.310 --> 00:47:22.080
That presence of someone
caring, as I mentioned.

00:47:22.080 --> 00:47:23.660
Think about positive empathy

00:47:23.660 --> 00:47:26.020
in addition to traditional empathy.

00:47:26.020 --> 00:47:28.420
Positive empathy is just
showing that you care

00:47:28.420 --> 00:47:30.000
to someone else.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:33.860
Giving a compliment, not a
fake compliment, a real one

00:47:33.860 --> 00:47:35.980
and you won't believe how far that will go

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:39.860
in terms of shifting their biochemicals.

00:47:39.860 --> 00:47:42.040
Maybe consider your best self.

00:47:42.040 --> 00:47:44.933
How do you wanna be seen and
talked about and experienced?

00:47:45.770 --> 00:47:50.770
Lastly, as you know, my work
is about emotion and arguing

00:47:51.290 --> 00:47:53.350
and I'll argue this until the end of time

00:47:53.350 --> 00:47:55.100
that it starts with giving
ourselves and others

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:57.750
the permission to be
our fulfilling selves.

00:47:57.750 --> 00:47:59.940
There's no such thing as a bad emotion.

00:47:59.940 --> 00:48:01.580
People have deemed that anxiety is bad

00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:03.060
and happiness is good.

00:48:03.060 --> 00:48:05.110
That's not a healthy way
of thinking about it,

00:48:05.110 --> 00:48:07.890
all emotions are experiences,
they're information.

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:11.180
Strive to be that emotion
scientist, not the judge.

00:48:11.180 --> 00:48:14.143
Remember, it's about
using our emotions wisely.

00:48:15.100 --> 00:48:17.640
I think we have to
appreciate that this domain

00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:22.640
of intelligence operates
and functions differently

00:48:22.690 --> 00:48:25.300
than traditional cognitive abilities.

00:48:25.300 --> 00:48:27.840
It's much harder as we've
seen in the pandemic.

00:48:27.840 --> 00:48:30.270
The levels of anxiety and
depression have been higher

00:48:30.270 --> 00:48:31.720
than we've seen in decades

00:48:33.260 --> 00:48:35.490
which means that it's demonstrating

00:48:35.490 --> 00:48:37.970
that it's hard to apply these skills,

00:48:37.970 --> 00:48:41.370
when the sand unfortunately
gets kicked in your face.

00:48:41.370 --> 00:48:42.760
And so we have to be mindful of that

00:48:42.760 --> 00:48:45.570
and I think be more prevention
focused, as I said earlier.

00:48:45.570 --> 00:48:47.590
Maybe be the role model for
your family and your friends

00:48:47.590 --> 00:48:50.490
and your colleagues, be open
to apologizing and forgiving

00:48:50.490 --> 00:48:52.920
and repairing, getting the
help that you might need,

00:48:52.920 --> 00:48:54.170
focus on systemic change.

00:48:54.170 --> 00:48:58.030
And I like to end in lofty ways

00:48:58.030 --> 00:49:00.080
just because that's the way I operate.

00:49:00.080 --> 00:49:02.720
I have a lot of hope for this work.

00:49:02.720 --> 00:49:06.730
I think that we're finally
as a nation taking seriously

00:49:06.730 --> 00:49:09.080
this other side of the report card.

00:49:09.080 --> 00:49:11.810
Still we have another couple
of decades to infuse it

00:49:11.810 --> 00:49:14.080
into the immune system of our nation

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:17.870
but going back to the
center's mission and vision,

00:49:17.870 --> 00:49:20.550
I think if we take seriously
our own and others'

00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:24.220
emotion skills, we can build a
healthier and more equitable,

00:49:24.220 --> 00:49:26.550
innovative and compassionate society.

00:49:26.550 --> 00:49:29.690
And as I run around the
world or I used to anyway

00:49:29.690 --> 00:49:32.410
and visiting schools, for
example, here in Connecticut,

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:35.160
some of the most high poverty
areas, I always ask myself,

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:39.130
will this child have the opportunities

00:49:39.130 --> 00:49:41.240
and learn the knowledge and the skills

00:49:41.240 --> 00:49:43.410
to achieve their dreams?

00:49:43.410 --> 00:49:47.750
And I'm still not convinced
that we're there yet.

00:49:47.750 --> 00:49:52.420
And so I'll end by saying, it's
really our moral obligation

00:49:52.420 --> 00:49:55.180
to take seriously our emotional lives

00:49:55.180 --> 00:49:57.210
and the emotional lives of others

00:49:57.210 --> 00:49:58.760
and I hope that this was enjoyable

00:49:58.760 --> 00:50:00.860
and let's take a couple
of minutes of questions.

00:50:00.860 --> 00:50:01.693
Thank you.

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:06.570
And if you want more information, oops,

00:50:06.570 --> 00:50:07.670
let me just skip that.

00:50:09.630 --> 00:50:12.660
That's the name of my book,
it's "Permission to Feel".

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:15.160
My website is where you can go
to learn more about the book

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:17.220
and also has links to our center

00:50:17.220 --> 00:50:19.460
and also to our school-based curriculum.

00:50:19.460 --> 00:50:21.210
So you can just go there
and it'll drive you

00:50:21.210 --> 00:50:24.700
to other resources and
on that note, thank you.

