00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:02.860
- Sure, first question
from the chat, thank you

00:51:02.860 --> 00:51:04.110
and thank you, Marc.

00:51:04.110 --> 00:51:08.130
Why would engagement with
both a high, low supervisor

00:51:08.130 --> 00:51:11.350
emotional intelligence be
so close on that graph?

00:51:11.350 --> 00:51:14.170
Seems like a low emotional
intelligence supervisor

00:51:14.170 --> 00:51:17.223
would be discouraging to be engaged.

00:51:18.512 --> 00:51:21.770
- That's a good question
and so I'm not 100% certain

00:51:21.770 --> 00:51:25.790
but what I would say is
that it just might be that

00:51:26.780 --> 00:51:31.780
for most people, if
they are like they care

00:51:33.740 --> 00:51:35.810
or they're interested
in their actual workload

00:51:35.810 --> 00:51:39.430
and whatever their job is
that it's less effected,

00:51:39.430 --> 00:51:41.570
it's slightly affected.

00:51:41.570 --> 00:51:46.180
But I think and maybe people
like to see themselves

00:51:46.180 --> 00:51:47.680
as being engaged in their work

00:51:47.680 --> 00:51:50.960
if there's a little bit
of method problems there.

00:51:50.960 --> 00:51:55.240
But what I think is more interesting is,

00:51:55.240 --> 00:51:58.200
or not more interesting
but I would say relevant

00:51:58.200 --> 00:52:00.940
is that engagement can also,

00:52:00.940 --> 00:52:03.320
we found in another study
that engagement and burnout

00:52:03.320 --> 00:52:05.750
are not inversely related.

00:52:05.750 --> 00:52:07.530
That there are people
who are highly engaged

00:52:07.530 --> 00:52:10.160
and also highly burnt out, about 20 to 25%

00:52:10.160 --> 00:52:12.693
of the U.S. workforce reports that.

00:52:12.693 --> 00:52:15.029
What we find is that those people are,

00:52:15.029 --> 00:52:16.540
they're doing their work

00:52:16.540 --> 00:52:18.380
but deep inside they're really burnt

00:52:18.380 --> 00:52:19.910
and they're looking for other jobs

00:52:19.910 --> 00:52:23.413
and so that engagement can
be masked also with burnout.

00:52:28.830 --> 00:52:29.910
- Another question.

00:52:29.910 --> 00:52:33.480
You so insightfully
talked about the necessity

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:37.000
of being able to properly
name our emotions.

00:52:37.000 --> 00:52:39.900
What to do then with children,
especially young children

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:43.680
because we wanna start them
as parents at a young age

00:52:43.680 --> 00:52:45.570
on the path to emotional intelligence.

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:48.620
So what are some of your
recommendations for parents

00:52:48.620 --> 00:52:51.100
and dealing with young children?

00:52:51.100 --> 00:52:53.160
- Yeah, so I don't know.

00:52:53.160 --> 00:52:55.620
I'm embarrassing to plug my book

00:52:55.620 --> 00:52:58.353
but that's a lot of my book is about that.

00:52:59.730 --> 00:53:01.980
But just to give you like the basics

00:53:01.980 --> 00:53:04.790
is A, you gotta be the role model.

00:53:04.790 --> 00:53:07.540
You have to be a parent
who is not gonna be

00:53:07.540 --> 00:53:11.050
that toxically positive
parent that's, dad is great.

00:53:11.050 --> 00:53:13.480
Or mommy is great,
everything's fine, right?

00:53:13.480 --> 00:53:18.140
A lot of people, we just
desperately want our kids

00:53:18.140 --> 00:53:21.240
to be happy all the time and
so we fake how we're feeling

00:53:21.240 --> 00:53:23.880
and we try to convince our
kids that they're happier

00:53:23.880 --> 00:53:25.310
than they actually are

00:53:25.310 --> 00:53:27.320
and I think that that's
a disservice to kids

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:29.140
when they're having their feelings.

00:53:29.140 --> 00:53:31.870
The best thing to do is
to validate those feelings

00:53:31.870 --> 00:53:35.080
and help them label them
properly and then find out,

00:53:35.080 --> 00:53:38.410
what they need to support
them in managing that feeling.

00:53:38.410 --> 00:53:42.570
And then you'll have kids
who have greater wellbeing.

00:53:42.570 --> 00:53:44.020
They won't necessarily
be happy all the time

00:53:44.020 --> 00:53:46.510
but I think the goal
for wellbeing might be

00:53:46.510 --> 00:53:48.710
actually better than
the goal for happiness.

00:53:49.930 --> 00:53:51.800
- And is that what your uncle Marvin did?

00:53:51.800 --> 00:53:54.280
Was that the side of the story?

00:53:54.280 --> 00:53:56.520
- He was like, gosh, you
know, you're being bullied.

00:53:56.520 --> 00:53:58.550
You've had terrible trauma in your life.

00:53:58.550 --> 00:54:01.060
Like it's understandable,
let's talk about it

00:54:01.060 --> 00:54:04.380
as opposed to let's ignore
it, try to get rid of it.

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:07.913
